This is a less bad version of #64263.

After #64115 was merged, I realized its approach had a surprising interaction with editor overlays when using the LSP-connected API. If you get an LSP-based snapshot and then do

```ts
const newSnapshot = lspSnapshot.update({
  fileSystem: createFileSystemLayer([
    [{ uri: "file:///home/andrew/project/src/main.ts" }, "// Updated content"]
  ])
});
```

you would clearly expect the `newSnapshot` to have the layer-provided contents for `main.ts`, but that will only be true if `main.ts` is not open in the editor.

#64115 works by creating a full `vfs.FS` view of the layer contents falling back to the host filesystem (if the layer is configured to fall back) which it passes to the project system, where `SnapshotFS` does its own layering: editor overlays on top of its cached files on top of the `vfs.FS` created by #64115, passed in by the API request. That's not the desired layer ordering, and a `vfs.FS` doesn’t provide enough info to split the actual API-provided layer apart from the host filesystem so the layers can be re-stacked as desired.

So, this PR refactors `requestfilesystem` to return a single layer providing the new `project.FileSystemLayer` interface that gives `SnapshotFS` enough info upon lookup to be able to stack that layer on top of the other layers it owns.

`_CustomizeMenu.scss` gives `.customize-menu` `visibility: hidden` and transitions `visibility 250ms` along with the slide-in `transform`, and `CustomizeMenu.jsx` focuses the close button from `onEntered`, which react-transition-group calls 250 ms after the panel starts opening.

Because `visibility` is transitioned, a style resolution taken at the transition's first sample still computes the from-value, `hidden`. Nothing in a `visibility: hidden` subtree has a focusable area, so `closeButtonRef.current.focus()` is a silent no-op and nothing retries: the panel stays open with focus wherever it was. For a keyboard or screen reader user, the customize panel opens and focus never enters it.

Normally a refresh tick lands inside the enter phase and the panel has computed `visible` long before `onEntered`. It does not when the content process's refresh driver is starved: react-transition-group forces only one style resolution, before any property has changed, so the transition is created at the next flush -- and if that flush is the one `focus()` itself forces, the transition is created and sampled at progress 0, `hidden`.

Reproduced in about:newtab by driving the panel the way the component does, but with no style flush during the enter phase:

    dialog.showModal();
    dialog.classList.add("customize-animate-enter");
    dialog.getBoundingClientRect();              // resolve style once while hidden
    dialog.classList.add("customize-animate-enter-done");
    closeButton.focus();                         // what onEntered does
    // getComputedStyle(dialog).visibility === "hidden", document.activeElement.id === ""

It is also how `browser_customize_menu_content.js` fails with `Close button should be focused when menu becomes visible - timed out after 50 tries` under `test-verify`, where the per-test profile shows no focus change was even attempted.

Fix: transition `visibility` only on the exit classes, the direction that needs it to keep the panel visible while it slides away. The panel then computes `visibility: visible` at the first style resolution of the enter phase, so the close button is focusable whenever `onEntered` runs. The animation does not change: the panel was `hidden` for one frame of the enter phase, during which the transform holds it off screen anyway.

Test dashboard: https://tests.firefox.dev/test.html?test=browser/extensions/newtab/test/browser/browser_customize_menu_content.js

In `_animateExpandedPinnedTabMove` in `drag-and-drop.js`, the check meant to center the dragged tab under the cursor compares the bare `screen`, which is `window.screen`, against pixel offsets:

```
if (
  screen < draggedTab.screenY + translateY ||
  screen > draggedTab.screenY + tabHeight + translateY
) {
  translateY = screen - draggedTab.screenY - tabHeight / 2;
}
```

Both comparisons are always false, so the branch never runs. It presumably meant the event's `screenY`. Found while converting the file to a module in bug 2080286.

On Android (Fenix Nightly and Beta 157) we have seen a generic activation failure bubble up from here:

    initialize@IPPChannelFilter.sys.mjs:181:13
    #startInternal@IPPProxyManager.sys.mjs:513:22

Desktop does not hit it.

`IPPChannelFilter.initialize` throws when the filter already has a `proxyInfo`:

    initialize(pass, server) {
      if (this.proxyInfo) {
        throw new Error("Double initialization?!?");
      }

and `IPPProxyManager.#startInternal` calls it after awaiting the server list, the auth provider and the pass:

    const server = lazy.IPProtectionServerlist.selectServer(location?.city);
    ...
    this.#connection.initialize(this.#pass, server);

So a `#startInternal` reaches this line while the connection created by `createChannelFilter()` was already initialized by an earlier run: either two activations overlap on the same filter, or the filter survived a failed activation. The Error object is not one of the typed `ERRORS` strings, so `#setErrorState` records it as `generic-error`, GeckoView maps the message to `ERROR_UNKNOWN`, and Fenix reports it as an unknown error.

Investigate why Android hits this path and desktop does not, e.g. whether the Fenix side can issue a second `GeckoView:IPProtection:Activate` (the location switch folds into the activation request) while one is in flight, or whether `cancelChannelFilter()` is skipped on an Android-only failure path.

[`#initSession` in SessionStore decides](https://searchfox.org/firefox-main/rev/b333202cd8d8385cb382c42404a03995dbc126ec/browser/components/sessionstore/SessionStore.sys.mjs#755) whether a deferred session has anything worth restoring right away with:

```js
if (iniState.windows.length || iniState.savedGroups) {
  state = iniState;
} else {
  state = null;
}
```

`iniState` comes from `#prepDataForDeferredRestore`, which builds it as:

```js
let defaultState = {
  windows: [],
  selectedWindow: 1,
  savedGroups: state.savedGroups || [],
};
```
...so `savedGroups` is always an array and always truthy, meaning the `else` branch can never be reached here. The comment immediately above the condition is also wrong and should be corrected at the same time - it says a window in `iniState` means "we have windows with pinned tabs to restore", but a window is kept for demoted closed tabs too, with no pinned tabs involved

It looks like the consequences of this are startup can take the restore path rather than the nothing-to-restore path:

- `sessionstore-restoring-on-startup` is notified when nothing will be restored.
- `session_restore.startup_timeline`'s `sessionRestoreRestoring` is set for a startup that restores nothing, so that milestone overcounts.
- `StartupPerformance` starts measuring on that notification, swaps to its `sessionstore-single-window-restored` observer, and then no window ever arrives. Its deadline timer is only started per restored window, so `_promiseFinished` never resolves and the observer stays attached for the life of the process. This is one route into the "restore produced nothing" case in bug 2071519.
- On Windows only, the all-windows-cloaked check in `#sendRestoreCompletedNotifications` runs, where on the intended path it would not.

Nothing hangs and nothing is user-visible: `#restoreWindows` short-circuits on an empty window list and notifies completion, and `#sendRestoreCompletedNotifications` resolves `#deferredAllWindowsRestored` with `#restoreCount` at 0.

